 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Speaking respectfully: ông, bà, cô and thưa",
     "texto": [
      "With your in-laws and with older people, **bạn** sounds too plain. A form of address is used depending on who the other person is:",
      "**ông**: to an older man (also to your father-in-law or a grandfather). «Chào ông» = hello, sir.",
      "**bà**: to an older or married woman (also to your mother-in-law or a grandmother). «Chào bà».",
      "**cô**: to a young woman, or to a female teacher. «Chào cô».",
      "**thưa** before a form of address adds respect: **Thưa bà, …** It is said when starting to speak or when answering: **Thưa không** = no, madam.",
      "• There is no «good morning» or «good afternoon»: **chào** + form of address works at any time of day.",
      "• **Rất hân hạnh được gặp ông** = it is an honour to meet you. It is the formal version of «Rất vui được gặp bạn».",
      "• **nhà tôi** (lit. «my house») = my husband or my wife. To ask about someone's partner: **Ông nhà có khỏe không?** = Is your husband well?"
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
